// tsx/typescript-03: two corrections to tsx/ts-b-scope that the scope rules of batch typescript-03 (no-unused-vars,
// no-use-before-define) share, until ts-b-scope takes them (NOTES-typescript-03.md).
//
// What a rule gets from this library:
//   variable(s, x, id, "using")   `using a = f()` and `await using a = f()` declare a, as `const` does
//   reexport_name(r)              r is a name in `export { r } from "m"`: another module's binding, no reference
//
// Examples, one per rule:
//   no-unused-vars          using res = open();                       res is a variable, assigned and never read
//   no-use-before-define    export type { A } from "./a"; import type { A } from "./a";   no use of the local A
//
// using. The TypeScript grammar now parses `using a = f()` as a using_declaration (tsx/statements' var_decl already
// reads it), but ts-b-scope declares variables from lexical_declaration only, so a `using` name was no variable and
// its uses resolved to nothing. Its declaring_stmt, initialised and space already take the kind "using".
// Re-exports. typescript-eslint's scope manager makes no reference for `export { a } from "m"` or
// `export { a as b } from "m"`: a is the other module's. ts-b-scope's use excludes the `name` of an export
// specifier only through export_local, which (rightly) holds only without a source, so these names were value
// uses that resolved to a local `a` when the file declares one.
//
// Builds on tsx/ts-b-scope.
#include "tsx/ts-b-scope.rerail"

variable(s, x, id, "using") :- kind(v, "using_declaration"), child(v, d, _), declarator_name(d, id, x),
    nearest(v, s, scope_node).

.decl reexport_name(r: node)
reexport_name(r) :- kind(sp, "export_specifier"), (field(sp, "name", r) ; field(sp, "alias", r)), kind(r, "identifier"),
    nearest(sp, e, "export_statement"), field(e, "source", _).
